William Thompson wrote:
> 
> Folks:
> 
> I have a few questions about DEC's new dtmail mail program delivered with the
> Common Desktop Environment that I hope somebody out there knows the answer to.
> I've been looking forward to support from DEC for MIME-based mail, but it looks
> like they've made it very difficult to move to this new mailer.
> 
> 1.  Is dtmail an mh-based mail program?  I was surprised to see that it didn't
> seem to recognize my already existing folders from dxmail.  Also, I use several
> mh-based mailers, including some which are usable from VT100 terminals (emacs,
> Pine), so conversion to a new format is not an option for me.  Another reason
> that conversion is not an option is that at least temporarily some of our
> computers will be running OSF v4 and will not have the CDE available to them,
> but they all share the same disks.

DtMail is a GUI (graphical user interface) to Digital UNIX's base mail
subsystem. This application allows the user to send, receive, and manage
mail messages. What makes DtMail different from the other base system
MUAs (Mail User Agents) is that it supports the MIME (Multipurpose
Internet Messages Extension) standard.
In addition, it is tightly integrated within CDE allowing the user to
perform operations such as drag-n-drop and inter application
communication. 

Message Storage
---------------
A major difference betweeen DXMail and DTMail is how messages are
stored. DXMail uses the folder structure provided by MH. This structure
consists of a folder (a directory in a UNIX file system) and messages
and/or subfolders. Messages are single files in numerical order within a
folder. In addition, a folder can contain
subfolders which is nothing else that subdirectories containing more
messages.

DtMail uses flat files as folders. These files contain multiple messages
seperated with a UNIX From line (Berkeley FROM Style format). Unlike
DXMail, DtMail does not impose a directory hierarchy on the user.
Folders can be scattered throught the users file system. 

Look and Feel
-------------
Users of DXMail will notice the main window of DtMail is somewhat
different than that of DXMail. 

In DXMail, the main window contains a view of the folders and the
message list for the opened folder. In DtMail, the main window contains
a message list for the opened folder and the text for the current
message. 

Also, the functions available to the user, via Buttons, from the main
window are not the same.

Folder View
-----------
DXMail provides a view into the users folder hiearchy from within the
application's main window. DtMail does not provide this view, but the
user can use CDE's File Manager to have a view into their folder
hierarchy. 

This is very important. If the user organizes its folders in a fashion
similar to MH's hierarchy, the user can use the powers of data actions
and data typing, in addition to the file manager, to create a similar
folder navigational look and feel as the one provided by DXMail. 

Features
--------
     Attachments 

     Capability of adding objects to your messages. These objects can be
sound,  	     video, audio, formatted documents, etc.

     Drag and Drop 

     Capability of dragging objects to and from the desktop. This is
powerfull           feature to use when copying text, and adding or
saving attachments.

     Signatures 

     Capability of adding a footer to your message describing yourself.
This is          where users add information such as company name,
telephone and fax number,         etc.

     Templates 

     Capability of creating preformated text which you constantly
include in             messages. This is good for filling out forms,
such as problem reports. You can      create a template which contains
data which never changes, such as your name,       telephone number,
email address, etc.

     Spell Checker 

     Capability of doing spell checking while you compose a message and
before you       hit the send button.

     Comprehensive Options Selection 

     A comprehensive list of run time options such as default folder
directory,          alias lists, timers, and much more.

 

If you would prefer not to change your message store format from the mh
format to the Berkeley From style format, but would like to have an MUA
that supports MIME, you could use exmh that is provided in the freeware
cd.

If however you would like to use Dtmail, you would need to convert your
mail storage, from MH folders to UNIX from folders by using the mailcv
program.

> 
> 2.  Is there a way to specify what editor to use within dtmail?  In dxmail,
> there's a nice Editor button which allows you to switch to the editor of your
> choice.  For me, that's emacs.

No, Dtmail does not provide a way to specify your editor of choice.


Hope the above information helps.

Jenny-Lou Sequeira



