In article <5tcku5$2b71@eccws1.dearborn.ford.com>,
patteeuw@sys2.ped.pto.ford.com wrote:

> DEC your driving me crazy !

<snip>

> The problem is certain Graphic Subsystems are only "supported" on certain
                                                      ^^^^^^^^^
> models of AlphaStations and Servers, even though (to the best of my
> knowledge) these all have PCI bus interfaces.  Some stations/servers
> "require" certain suffix versions that I have never even seen !!!

<snip>
 
> Come on DEC, why can't all PCI bused Alpha's use all of "your" PCI bus
> Graphics Subsystems ??!!!!!

    Jack -

    The key word here is "supported:" many of the newer revs of boards
will work on the older workstations/servers, but have not been around long
enough for Digital to be able to claim "support." There may be issues with
specific hardware combinations that will never arise with your hardware,
or you may be unlucky.

    You are right, though, in noting that Digital frequently EOL's
graphics cards before announcing that they support some "older" (e.g. 1 -
1.5 year old) models. Generally speaking, if all they changed is the "Y"
VVVVV-XY (last character only; e.g., PB2GA-JC/JD replacing -JA/JB in your
example), AND the product description is identical, you're OK. We've been
having the same problem with 10/100 Ethernet and FDDI-TP adapters: in the
space of 2 - 4 months, all the part numbers change, and even DEC Direct
tech support had little or no idea what really replaces what, except the
above rule of thumb.
All I can say is that it's worked so far.

    Fortune favors the brave.

    Hoep this helps,

                  Joe Gurman

