On Mon, 04 Aug 1997 21:55:12 -0500, magrasjc@texas.net wrote:

>Greetings everyone,
>
>I am trying to get the following code to compile on a g++ 2.7.2.2
>and libg++ 2.7.2 on DEC UNIX 3.2... but I cant discern what the
>external function defintion's header should look like...
>
>Thanks in advance for your help,
>
>J.C. Magras
>
>#include <function> // yields unary_function template
>
>***** given this generic template
>template<class Protocol,class Result>
>class jack:pubic unary_function<Protocol, Result>
>{
>public:
>Result& jack<Protocol&>::operator(Protocol&){};
>};
>
>***** given this template specialization
>template<class pTCPIP,class pTCPIP>
>class jack<pTCPIP,pTCPIP>:public unary_function<Protocol,Result>
>{
>friend class pTCPIP;
>public:
>  Result& jack<Protocol>::operator()( Protocol &)    ;
>};
>************
>what is the function signature/header for:
>pTCPIP&  jack :: operator() (pTCPIP & e) { } ?

I haven't got a copy of g++ to try this on but it compiles with the
DEC CXX compiler.

Your main problem is that the syntax of your specialised class is
incorrect. I have highlighted the differences with comments.

// functional required on dec compiler.
#include <functional> // yields unary_function template

// I presume you meant public and not pubic :-)
template<class Protocol,class Result>
class jack : public unary_function<Protocol, Result> {
public:
// presumably operator() - a set of parentheses were missing 
// in the original code
   Result& jack<Protocol&>::operator()(Protocol&) {};
};

class pTCPIP;

// note syntax for specialising template class 
// i.e. no template<...> construct
class jack<pTCPIP, pTCPIP> {
friend class pTCPIP;
public:
// no need to explicitly scope the operator. If you did, use:
//    jack<pTCPIP, pTCPIP>:: ... not jack<Protocol>::
// specify the concrete type, not Result for ret val.
    pTCPIP& operator()(pTCPIP&);
};

// Define the function out of line:
pTCPIP& jack<pTCPIP, pTCPIP>::operator()(pTCPIP& p) {
   return p; // do whatever
}

Martin.

