In article <33f0b62e.0@oit.umass.edu>, krotkov@emily.oit.umass.edu (Peter M Krotkov) writes:

>I have an OpenVMS Alpha Software Product Library CD that I'd like to
>mount on a DU 4.0B box.  Alas, I cannot.  The cd's are evidently
>mastered in files-11/ods-2 (vms) format.  Any clues?

You are correct about the format.  Digital UNIX doesn't have file
system support for that particular file system.  Your choices would
seem to be:

o  Put it on a VMS where it can be read.

o  Write a program that can read that file system format.  There
   is a book on the file system internals.  May still be in print.
   At the time it was probably published by Digital Press.  Probably
   still is.

># mount -r /dev/rz4c /mnt
>/dev/rz4c on /mnt: No valid filesystem exists on this partition

This is trying to mount UFS.  It isn't UFS...
>
># mount -rt cdfs /dev/rz4c /mnt
>cdfs_mount: Unsupported disk format
>/dev/rz4c on /mnt: No valid filesystem exists on this partition

And it isn't ISO-9660.
>
># disklabel -r /dev/rrz4c  -- 4a reports the same error
>Disk is unlabeled or, /dev/rrz4c does not start at block 0 of the disk

And it doesn't have a Digital UNIX disklabel.  It is an OpenVMS disk
after all.
