Newsgroups: gnu.g++.help,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.programming


I have found that for template class definitions in g++, you must
put the implementation in the .h file.
For non-template classes, you must *not* put the definition in the .h
file, otherwise you get multiple defns.

For other compilers, like DEC CXX, you must not put the implementation
in the .h file for any kind of class, unless it's inline.


Kevin

PS. Presumably you are using the guarded include trick?

#ifndef FOO_H
#define FOO_H
 
header follows

#endif



