>#  else
>#   define _Pfield(_arg_)       __/**/_arg_
>#  endif
># endif
>
>typedef struct __pthread_attr_t {
>    long                _Pfield(valid);
>    char*               _Pfield(name);
>    unsigned long       _Pfield(arg);
>    unsigned long       _Pfield(reserved)[19];
>    } pthread_attr_t;
>
>I hacked it until things started compiling by removing all the
>defines and only defining #define _Pfield(_arg_) _arg_.  This
>seems to make it work.  Defining the one with __/**/_arg_ brings
>on the error.
>
>Anybody know what the problem is exactly?
>

This is a known problem with versions of Pro*C up to and including the one
that shipped in RDBMS 7.3.2.  I believe it was fixed in 7.3.3.    I'm not
sure if a fix/workaround  is available for older Pro*C's, to find out you
probably need to contact Oracle support.

The problem does have to do with the use of the empty comment /**/.  
Pro*C really doesn't like this, and unfortunately Digital UNIX 4.0X
started using it in its system headers.

--Tony
tquan@ibm.net



