In article <5sfgat$d6j@epx.cis.umn.edu>, Graham Allan <allan@spartha.spa.umn.edu> wrote:


> I have a question regarding password encryption under DU's enhanced
> security. For some reason I was under the impression that enhanced security
> used a different encryption scheme for the passwords stored in the prpasswd file,
> and now I have been led to doubt it. I can't find any clear answer in the online
> manuals. Could anyone give me a quick answer?

Digital Unix 4.0, at least, can use either bigcrypt or plain old crypt,
depending up the settings in the system and user profiles. Indeed, you
can set things up (as we have),so that passwords inherited from a non-enhanced
system use crypt, but when one changes their password, it uses bigcrypt.
This lets us deal with legacy password that people though were larger
than 8 characters.

