 I just had the same experience with DEC UNIX V4.0b.  If you want to fix the
problem without having to install the whole patch you can do so by setting
the kernel variable "db_enable" to 0 (it defaults to 1 if you have KDEBUG
defined in the kernel's configuration file).

Use dbx(1) to patch the running kernel, and/or  the on-disk copy.  For the
running kernel use:

        dbx -k /vmunix                        # or whatever the current
kernel's name is
        assign db_enable = 0
        quit

For the on-disk copy of the kernel, leave off the "-k" option and use the
"patch" sub command instead of "assign", eg:

        dbx /vmunix                        # or whatever the current
kernel's name is
        patch db_enable = 0
        quit

[Please excuse any illegibility in this post:  I'm using a new Newsreader
for the first time, and it's deciding how I want my message formatted for
me...]

-- Sam

